WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:21.908
A tap that drips is not about to fail. It has already failed, and every drop is going down the drain
paid for. Closing it harder stops the noise for a day and drives the damage deeper into the tap.

2
00:00:22.408 --> 00:00:38.708
Decide that before you touch a spanner. A washer tap screws down: the handle turns several
times and it closes by pressing rubber onto brass. That is the tap this film repairs.

3
00:00:38.928 --> 00:00:58.340
A washerless tap moves a quarter turn, or is a single lever, and inside it is a cartridge, a disc pack
or a ball. It has no washer to change, so this procedure does not apply and you fit a kit instead.

4
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:10.729
Turn the water off at the service valve under the fitting if there is one, and
at the main stop tap if there is not. Shut the hot supply at the heater as well.

5
00:01:10.949 --> 00:01:25.577
Then open the tap and let it run dry. That is not a formality. It is the only proof you
have shut the right valve, and it costs you ten seconds against a room full of water.

6
00:01:26.077 --> 00:01:39.220
Look at where the water is coming from, because there are two faults and they are
not the same. Water from the spout is the washer or the seat: the tap is not closing.

7
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:55.462
Water around the handle is a weep at the stem, and that is the gland packing or the O-ring.
Strip a tap for the wrong one and you will fit a new washer to a fault that was never there.

8
00:01:55.962 --> 00:02:12.169
Prise or unscrew the cap in the middle of the handle. Under it is one screw. Take it out, and the
handle lifts off the stem. If it is stuck, put the screw back a couple of turns and pull against that.

9
00:02:12.389 --> 00:02:27.993
Now slacken the gland nut and thread the whole stem out by turning it in the OPEN
direction. Pad your plier jaws with tape before they touch chrome, and go gently on plastic.

10
00:02:28.493 --> 00:02:43.586
On the end of the stem is the washer, held by a brass screw. A washer that has gone hard,
or has a groove pressed into it, is your drip. Undo the screw and take the old one off.

11
00:02:43.806 --> 00:02:59.038
Take the old washer with you and match it for size and style. Guessing gets you one that almost
fits, which leaks. If the brass screw is chewed, renew that too, in brass and not in steel.

12
00:02:59.538 --> 00:03:14.585
If this tap eats washers every few months, the washer is not the fault. The seat is: the
brass ring the washer closes onto has been cut, and it is cutting each new washer in turn.

13
00:03:14.805 --> 00:03:34.588
Shine a torch down the body. A square, hexagonal or slotted hole means the seat unscrews, and a seat wrench
turns it out anticlockwise. A plain round hole means it does not, and you dress it in place until it is smooth.

14
00:03:35.088 --> 00:03:58.262
For the other fault, renew the gland packing. Wrap one turn around the stem under the nut, in the direction the nut tightens,
so that doing the nut up winds the packing in rather than unwinding it. Some stems take an O-ring instead: match it and fit it.

15
00:03:58.762 --> 00:04:09.396
Back together in the reverse order of taking it apart, with a smear
of silicone grease on the stem threads so the handle turns sweetly.

16
00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:30.421
The gland nut goes hand tight and then half a turn more. Not as hard as it will go: over-tighten it and the handle
becomes stiff and the packing is crushed. Turn the water back on slowly and watch both places before you walk away.

17
00:04:30.921 --> 00:04:51.262
So: identify the kind. Isolate, and prove it dry. Read which leak it is. Strip it in order, padded. Washer
and seat for a spout drip, packing for a stem weep. Then reverse, greased, hand tight and a half turn.
